Q: IConfiguration.Get&lt;&gt;() not retrieving for elements with properties with empty arrays A: <p>I was able to reproduce your problem using .NET 10, see failing demo fiddle #1 <a href="https://dotnetfiddle.net/ad1T9R" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>.  The issue seems to be with binding to types that have parameterized constructors, as I was also able to reproduce the exact same behavior using an equivalent parameterized class:</p>
<pre><code>public sealed class ServerProcessConfig // Also does not bind when Dependencies has Length zero.
{
    public ServerProcessConfig(int Id, int[]? Dependencies) =&gt; (this.Id, this.Dependencies) = (Id, Dependencies);

    public int Id { get; }
    public int[]? Dependencies { get; }
}
</code></pre>
<p>See failing demo fiddle #2 <a href="https://dotnetfiddle.net/HNMzje" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>.</p>
<p>As a workaround, I'd suggest replacing the parameterized properties with
<code>public required ... { get; init; }</code> properties like so:</p>
<pre><code>public sealed record ServerProcessConfig
{
    public required int Id { get; init; }
    public required int[]? Dependencies { get; init; }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Using this version of <code>ServerProcessConfig</code>, your settings array is bound successfully.  (A class with the same properties will work also.)</p>
<p>See working demo fiddle #3 <a href="https://dotnetfiddle.net/7ArvIO" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>.</p>
<p>And, as noted by <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/6908545/ivan-petrov">Ivan Petrov</a> in comments, adding a parameterless constructor also works around the problem:</p>
<pre><code>public sealed record ServerProcessConfig(
    int Id,
    int[]? Dependencies)
{
    public ServerProcessConfig() : this(-1, null) { }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The only possible disadvantage with this approach is that the positional properties are no longer required, so the following now compiles:</p>
<pre><code>var test1 = new ServerProcessConfig { Id = 101 }; // Note - still seems to compile  even without setting Dependencies
</code></pre>
<p>Working fiddle #4 <a href="https://dotnetfiddle.net/vwfvgB" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>.</p>
<p>You might consider reporting an issue about this to Microsoft; binding to types with parameterized constructors that have array-valued arguments is clearly supported in general, so it is strange that binding fails when the array has zero length.</p>

